#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229982} :activity/id #uuid "ecc6f7d5-a6a0-5418-9648-573635651982" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050707 :topic/id #uuid "605a21e1-c415-5cc1-9181-c8f48db5f4ba" :topic/math-academy-id 6691} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230191 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving *inhomogeneous systems* of ODEs as well. Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, and suppose we are given an initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\n\nOur goal is to derive the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the *inhomogeneous term* by a change of variables. We define\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand differentiate it using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =\\cancel{-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+\\cancel{A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate both sides from $t_0$ to $t$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to the initial variable $\\mathbf{x}(t).$ To do that we multiply both sides by $e^{A(t-t_0)}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our IVP is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere the first term represents the solution of the corresponding *homogeneous system*, and the second term gives a particular solution of our *inhomogeneous system*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "ff1bfa7f-286f-5481-8170-effabae1aa53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14524 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e2fe55d-42e2-5c03-89a2-e19a648504d7" :step/math-academy-id 589676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228916 :question/id #uuid "e151cd67-cf96-5378-9380-e9960c1eb6b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050221}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479854}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"} :step/id #uuid "77745a11-4271-51f5-b398-2f10162b46cb" :step/math-academy-id 589677 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229984 :step/content {:db/id #e